ains here  we get 3x squared plus 3x h plus h  squared minus 4 all divided by H you can  see those HS are cancelling but remember  it's the limit as H approaches 0 right  so if we put 0 in now for H this whole  term is going to go to 0 this whole term  is going to go to 0 and we're left with  3 x squared minus 4 is our derivative  okay that's a formula for our slope but  what we want to do is we want to find  out where that slope is 0 okay that's  where the graph is you know turning  around or bending and so what we're  going to do is we're going to add 4 to  both sides okay so that's 3 x squared  we're gonna divide both sides by 3  so that's  four-thirds we're going to take the  square root of both sides that gives us  plus or minus and I did this on the  calculator for us it's 1.15 okay now if  we put 1.15 back into our original  equation what we get is 1.15 comma  negative three point zero eight and if  we put a negative 1.15 we get positive  three point zero eight so those are our  turning points so if we graph this you  can see that negative one point one five  we're going to be up here at let's see  we're gonna be at three point zero eight  so it's gonna be right about there and  if we're at positive one point five  we're gonna be right about there so that  gives us a better idea okay of how high  the graph is going and how low the graph  is going otherwise we're just kind of  guessing we don't really know where  those extra Mar those maximums and those  minimums right so that's a key point and  again you can see where that graph is  bending or turning or changing direction  that's where the slope of the tangent  line is equal to zero so key points to  remember when you're learning about  derivatives is you know what is a  derivative it's really a formula for the  slope how do we find the slope we take  these two points okay and we use our  slope formula but we take it a step  further to go from the average rate of  change to the instantaneous rate of  change 